    description: "Spells out what the firm will do, what it costs, when it is paid and how the work can end.",
    reviewNote: "BEFORE THE CLIENT SIGNS: (1) List the services and the fee exactly as quoted; the fee must be flat, monthly or hourly, never a share of a refund. (2) If interest on overdue invoices is switched on, tell the client the rate; the form will not accept more than 0.67% a month. (3) Have the client initial the electronic-delivery section (or switch it off), then give the client a signed copy. DETAIL: Checked against the primary text on 2026-10-14. Circular 230 (31 CFR Part 10): no contingent fee for work before the IRS, which includes preparing returns, and no unconscionable fee (§10.27(a)-(c)), so fees here are flat, monthly or hourly only and never a share of a refund. Client records must be returned promptly on request even if a fee is unpaid; the one exception is a return or document the firm itself prepared and is holding until the fee for that document is paid (§10.28(a)-(b)), so this agreement promises only the client's own documents and the records the rule requires. The firm may rely in good faith on client information without verifying it but must ask reasonable questions when it looks incorrect, inconsistent or incomplete (§10.34(d)); must tell the client promptly of a known error or omission and its consequences (§10.21); must not sign a return with a position that lacks a reasonable basis (§10.34(a)); must tell the client of likely penalties (§10.34(c)); and may not endorse a federal refund check or direct or accept a federal refund into an account it controls (§10.31). All of this is in the client-responsibilities clause. E-file: the client signs the IRS e-file signature authorization (Form 8879 for an individual) after reviewing the return and before the firm transmits it (IRS Pub. 1345). Interest on overdue invoices: Maryland's interest statute regulates a 'loan'; no separate Maryland rule for interest on an unpaid service invoice was found. If an invoice balance were treated as a loan, Commercial Law §12-102 caps interest at 6% a year and §12-103(a) allows 8% under a written agreement signed by the borrower, so the default of 0.5% a month (6% a year, simple interest) is safe either way and the maximum accepted is 0.67% a month (8% a year). Interest on a court judgment (10% a year) is a different rule (Courts Art. §11-107). Electronic delivery and signing: Maryland's UETA applies only between parties who agreed, and in a standard-form contract that term must be conspicuous and separately consented to (Commercial Law §21-104(b)(3)); it is therefore a separate section the client initials, and it carries the statements the federal E-SIGN Act requires before a consumer consents (15 USC §7001(c)). Tax return information may be shared without a separate consent only as 26 CFR §301.7216-2 allows (other U.S. preparers, software and e-file providers, state returns, payment); anything else needs the separate Authorization to Release Information form (IRC §7216). File retention: a signing preparer must keep a copy or list of each return for 3 years after the end of the return period (July 1 to June 30) in which it was presented for signature (26 CFR §1.6107-1(b)); the 7-year default is the firm's own choice. Not included on purpose, because they are business-risk choices: a cap on the firm's liability, a shortened time to sue and arbitration; add them in the additional-terms box only if you want them. Added on the fourth pass: a no-audit and no-legal-advice sentence, a statement that taxes are the client's to pay on time, a fair share of a flat fee if the work ends early, and joint responsibility when more than one person signs as the client.",
