    description: "Lets the firm share specific client records with a named person or company. (For IRS or Maryland tax representation use the 2848 / 8821 / 548 tools.)",
    reviewNote: "BEFORE THE CLIENT SIGNS: (1) Fill in every blank first (who gets the information, what information, why, how long). A consent signed blank and filled in later is not valid (Rev. Proc. 2013-14 §5.04(4)). (2) Leave the income-tax-return switch on if anything listed was given to the firm for, or used in, an income tax return; print this form alone (nothing else on the page) and give the client a copy. (3) Never make signing it a condition of doing the client's work; if the client says no, do the work anyway. DETAIL: Rebuilt on 2026-10-14 from the primary text: 26 CFR §301.7216-3 and Rev. Proc. 2013-14 (the IRS text is copied word for word, with the firm's name where the IRS text says 'we' or 'our', as §5.04(1) allows). When it is needed: IRC §7216 covers 'tax return information', which means information given to the firm for or in connection with preparing an INCOME tax return (§301.7216-1(b)(1), (b)(3)); a payroll, sales-tax or other non-income-tax filing, or bookkeeping that was never used for an income tax return, is outside §7216, so with the switch off the form is a plain release (still signed and dated). If in doubt, leave the switch on. No consent is needed to disclose return information to the IRS, under a court order or subpoena, to other U.S. preparers or e-file/software providers who help prepare or file the return, for the related state returns, to process payment, or to keep records (§301.7216-2). Consent rules: the consent must name the preparer and the taxpayer, state the purpose, name the recipient, describe the information, and be signed and dated before anything is disclosed; a signature on a blank form that is filled in afterward is not valid (Rev. Proc. 2013-14 §5.04(4)); services cannot be conditioned on it (§301.7216-3(a)(1)); disclosure and use need separate documents, so this form covers disclosure only (§301.7216-3(c)(1)); a consent for the entire return must say the taxpayer can ask for less (§301.7216-3(c)(2)); the taxpayer gets a copy at signing (§301.7216-3(c)(3)). Duration: a consent may state its own duration and, if it does not, lasts one year from signing (§301.7216-3(b)(5)); the old 'until I cancel' choice is now 'one year from the date I sign' so the period is always stated. TIGTA: the telephone and email wording is the exact text of Rev. Proc. 2013-14 §5.04(1)(d). Form 1040 filers (Rev. Proc. 2013-14 §5.02-5.03): a paper consent must be on letter-size paper or larger, contain only this authorization and be in at least 12-point type, so print this one alone; the PDF and the Word file both print it at 12 points. The consent must be affirmative (nothing pre-ticked) and, if signed electronically, the taxpayer must type their own name or enter a PIN of 5 or more characters that the software did not fill in (§6.02). For businesses (not Form 1040) any readable format works if the §301.7216-3(a)(3)(i) items are present, and this form meets them. Outside the United States: this form is for disclosures to third parties other than a preparer helping prepare the return; if the recipient is a tax return preparer outside the United States, a different statement is required and a Form 1040 filer's Social Security number may not be sent unless the safeguards in §301.7216-3(b)(4) are met, so do not use this form for that. It is not an IRS 2848/8821 or Maryland Form 548.",
