    description: "Defines how partners share capital, profits, decisions and authority, what happens if a partner leaves or dies, and how the partnership winds up.",
    reviewNote: "STAFF — CHECK THESE FIRST: (1) Tell the partners in plain words that each partner can be personally responsible for ALL of the business's debts, and ask whether an LLC would suit them better. If the business name ends in LLC, Inc. or Corp., this is the wrong document. (2) The profit shares total 100 and the capital amounts are right. (3) A partner can always leave; a shorter notice than the one chosen is a breach, not a bar. Make sure the partners understand the notice period and the buyout choice. (4) Licenses (tobacco, lottery, alcohol, SNAP, health), the lease and loans may need notice or consent when partners change. GET A LAWYER IF: more than three partners; a partner is a company or a trust; real estate, a franchise or an alcohol or lottery license is in the partnership's name; partners put in very unequal capital; or a partner lives outside Maryland. DETAIL FOR THE FILE: In a general partnership each partner can be personally responsible for ALL of the business's debts, jointly and severally, not only the partner's share (Corporations and Associations Article §9A-306(a)). Points settled from the statute and regulations: (1) A partner may withdraw at any time, rightfully or wrongfully, by express will (§9A-601(1), §9A-602(a)); an agreement cannot take that power away, and may only require the notice to be in writing (§9A-103(b)(6)). Withdrawing in breach of the agreement, such as on less notice than it requires, is wrongful and makes the partner liable for the damages the withdrawal causes (§9A-602(b)(1), (c)); this form requires the notice period chosen in the questions and limits damages to actual losses. In a partnership at will, a partner's notice of withdrawal by itself dissolves the partnership unless the agreement says otherwise (§9A-801(1)); this form says the remaining partners may elect to continue and buy the departing partner out within 30 days, and winds up if they do not. (2) A partner is also dissociated, unless the agreement says otherwise, on death, on the appointment of a guardian or general conservator, on a court's finding that the partner has become incapable of performing the partner's duties under the agreement, and on bankruptcy or assignment for creditors (§9A-601(6), (7)) — the form defines 'incapacitated' in exactly those terms. (3) Expulsion 'pursuant to the partnership agreement' is allowed (§9A-601(3)), so the optional removal clause works; a court can still expel a partner for wrongful conduct, willful or persistent material breach, or conduct that makes it not reasonably practicable to carry on with the partner, and an agreement cannot change that (§9A-601(5), §9A-103(b)(7)). (4) The agreement cannot eliminate the duty of loyalty or unreasonably reduce the duty of care or the duty of good faith and fair dealing, cannot unreasonably restrict access to the books, cannot change the court's power to wind up the partnership for illegality or on a partner's or transferee's application, and cannot restrict third parties' rights (§9A-103(b)); the duties are limited to those in §9A-404 (loyalty: account for benefits, no adverse dealing, no competing before dissolution; care: no grossly negligent or reckless conduct, intentional misconduct or knowing violation of law), and the duty not to compete ends when the partner is dissociated (§9A-603(b)). (5) If a dissociation does not dissolve the partnership, the partnership must buy the departing partner's interest at the amount that would be distributable if the assets were sold at the greater of liquidation value or going-concern value, with interest from the date of dissociation, less damages and other amounts the partner owes, indemnify the departing partner against partnership liabilities, and pay an estimated price within 120 days after a written demand if no agreement is reached (§9A-701); this form uses fair market value of the assets (with or without goodwill, as chosen) and a 90-day payment term — a partner who thinks that is less than the statute gives should talk to an attorney. A statement of dissociation can be filed with the State Department of Assessments and Taxation and, after 90 days, outsiders are treated as knowing of the departure (§9A-704, §9A-105). (6) Partnership tax — capital accounts follow Treas. Reg. §1.704-1(b)(2)(iv) and liquidating distributions follow positive capital account balances; a partner with a negative balance after losses must pay it in, as §9A-807(b) also requires; payments for work that do not depend on partnership income are guaranteed payments under IRC §707(c). (7) IRS audits — see the explanation in the LLC Operating Agreement note: a partnership representative with a substantial U.S. presence is designated each year (IRC §6223(a); Treas. Reg. §301.6223-1), an imputed underpayment is assessed against the partnership at the highest rate unless the partnership elects within 45 days after the notice of final adjustment to push it out to the reviewed-year partners (IRC §§6225, 6226), and the optional election out (IRC §6221(b); Treas. Reg. §301.6221(b)-1) needs 100 or fewer K-1s (counting an S corporation partner's shareholders) and only individuals, C or S corporations or a deceased partner's estate as partners — a trust, partnership, nominee or one-owner LLC partner rules it out. (8) The partnership statute (Title 9A) has no limit on distributions of its own; the sentence in the profits, losses and pay section is the partners' own rule. (9) Filings: a partnership using a name other than its partners' own names should register the name with the State Department of Assessments and Taxation; a general partnership files SDAT Form 2 (personal property tax return) each year by April 15. (10) Licenses (tobacco, lottery, alcohol, SNAP, health), the lease and any loan may need notice or consent when partners change. (11) The profit-share percentages must total 100 — the software blocks saving otherwise. A business whose name ends in LLC or Corp is not a general partnership: if the owners formed an LLC use the LLC Operating Agreement. (12) Spouse page — no Maryland statute was found that requires a spouse's consent to a partnership transfer (dower is abolished, E&T §3-202, and Maryland is not a community-property state); the page only makes the spouse promise not to claim to be a partner, and does not stop a divorce court from awarding the spouse money based on marital property (Family Law §§8-201(e), 8-205) or take away rights in the estate. (13) The optional operating-partner clause moves responsibility between the partners only; a government agency or creditor can still collect from the partnership or any partner, and a SNAP, tobacco or lottery license may have its own rules about who may operate under it. Limits on one partner's authority bind the partners among themselves, but an outsider who does not know of them can still hold the partnership to a partner's act in its ordinary business. (14)",
