    body: `INDEPENDENT CONTRACTOR AGREEMENT

This Agreement is made on {{effectiveDate}} between {{company}}{{#if companyAddress}}, {{companyAddress}}{{/if}} ("Company") and {{contractor}}{{#if contractorAddress}}, {{contractorAddress}}{{/if}} ("Contractor").

1. SERVICES
Contractor will perform the following services: {{services}}

2. TERM
Work begins on {{startDate}}{{#if hasEnd}} and ends on {{endDate}}, unless ended earlier under this Agreement{{else}} and continues until either party ends it as described in this Agreement{{/if}}.

3. PAYMENT
Company will pay Contractor {{#if feeType=fixed}}a fixed fee of {{fee}} for the services{{else}}{{fee}} per hour for time worked{{/if}}. Contractor will send invoices{{#if feeType=hourly}} listing the dates and hours worked{{/if}} and Company will pay each within {{payDays|day}} of receiving it. Contractor pays Contractor's own expenses unless Company agrees in writing in advance to reimburse them.

4. INDEPENDENT CONTRACTOR
Contractor is an independent contractor, not an employee. {{#if indBusiness}}Contractor runs their own business and has other customers or is free to take them. {{/if}}{{#if indSchedule}}Contractor sets their own schedule and methods; Company controls the result of the work, not the means. {{/if}}{{#if indTools}}Contractor supplies their own tools and may use helpers at their own cost. {{/if}}{{#if indAway}}The work is done away from Company's premises or outside Company's usual day-to-day business. {{/if}}{{#if indInsurance}}Contractor carries any insurance and workers' compensation coverage the law requires for Contractor and Contractor's helpers. {{/if}}Any helpers Contractor uses are Contractor's responsibility, not Company's. Contractor has no authority to bind Company. Company will not withhold taxes or provide benefits, except that Company may withhold where the law requires it (for example, backup withholding if Contractor does not provide a taxpayer identification number on Form W-9). Contractor holds, and will keep, every license and permit the work requires, is responsible for their own taxes and insurance, and will provide a completed Form W-9 before the first payment; Company will report payments on Form 1099-NEC where required.{{#if constructionWork}} Because this work is construction or landscaping work, Maryland's Workplace Fraud Act (Labor and Employment Article, Title 3, Subtitle 9) applies. Company will give Contractor, at the time of hiring, the written notice of classification that the Act requires, in English and Spanish, and Contractor's signature below confirms receipt of it. If Contractor works through a business entity, that entity acknowledges that Maryland law makes it responsible to withhold, report and pay payroll taxes for all of its employees, to pay unemployment insurance taxes for them, and to maintain workers' compensation insurance; and Contractor will give Company, on request, a signed statement that Contractor is an independent contractor available to work for other businesses, a current certificate of status from the Maryland State Department of Assessments and Taxation, and proof of every license the work requires.{{/if}}

{{#if confidential}}5. CONFIDENTIALITY
Contractor will keep Company's non-public business, client and financial information confidential and use it only for this work, during and after this Agreement. Nothing in this Agreement stops Contractor from reporting a possible violation of law to a government agency. Under 18 U.S.C. § 1833(b), an individual is not liable under any trade-secret law for disclosing a trade secret in confidence to a government official or an attorney solely to report or investigate a suspected violation of law, or in a sealed court filing.

{{/if}}6. OWNERSHIP OF WORK
{{#if ownsWork}}Contractor assigns to Company, by signing this Agreement, all rights in the work Contractor creates specifically for Company under this Agreement, effective when Company has paid for it. The parties intend this signed assignment, and not a "work made for hire" arrangement, to transfer ownership. Contractor will sign any further paper Company reasonably needs to confirm or record the transfer. Contractor promises that the work is Contractor's own or properly licensed and, to Contractor's knowledge, does not infringe anyone else's rights. Contractor keeps rights in Contractor's own pre-existing tools and materials and gives Company a license to use any of them built into the work.{{else}}Contractor keeps ownership of the work Contractor creates under this Agreement and, once Company has paid for it, gives Company a non-exclusive license to use the work for Company's own business.{{/if}}

7. ENDING THE AGREEMENT
Either party may end this Agreement on 14 days' written notice, or on written notice if the other party materially breaks it and does not fix the problem within 7 days of that notice (or at once, if the problem cannot be fixed). Company pays for work completed up to the end date; for a fixed fee, the part of the fee that matches the work completed. When the Agreement ends, Contractor returns Company's property, records and materials.

8. RESPONSIBILITY FOR CLAIMS
Each party is responsible for claims, losses and costs caused by its own negligence or misconduct. Contractor is also responsible for claims by Contractor's helpers, and for taxes, penalties and insurance that the law requires of Contractor.

9. GENERAL
This is the parties' entire agreement, may be changed only in writing signed by both, and is governed by the laws of the State of {{state}}. If any part of it cannot be enforced, the rest stays in force. Notices must be in writing and sent to the party's address at the top of this Agreement, or to a new address the party gives in writing. Neither party may assign this Agreement without the other's written consent. The sections on payment, {{#if confidential}}confidentiality, {{/if}}ownership of work and responsibility for claims continue after this Agreement ends. This Agreement may be signed in copies, including electronically, and all copies together are one agreement.{{#if mediation}} Before going to court (other than to stop misuse of confidential information), the parties will try in good faith to settle a dispute through mediation and will share the mediator's fee equally.{{/if}}

{{#if additionalTerms}}10. ADDITIONAL TERMS
{{additionalTerms}}

{{/if}}COMPANY: {{company}}
Signature: ______________________________   Date: ______________
If a company — By: ______________________________   Name: ______________________________   Title: ______________________________

CONTRACTOR: {{contractor}}
Signature: ______________________________   Date: ______________
If a company — By: ______________________________   Name: ______________________________   Title: ______________________________`,
