    reviewNote: "CHECK BEFORE HANDING IT OVER: (1) Facts and dates only — no opinions and no guesses about motives. (2) The issue must not involve the use of sick or safe leave, an injury or workers' compensation claim, a pay complaint or pay discussion, medical information, a report to a government agency, or a protected trait (race, sex, age, religion, national origin, disability, pregnancy). (3) If the handbook lists steps of progressive discipline, follow them. (4) Give the employee a copy and keep one; if the employee refuses to sign, a witness notes it on the form. GET A LAWYER IF: the employee recently complained, asked for leave or an accommodation, was hurt or is pregnant; the employee has a written contract or a union; or this is a final warning likely to lead to firing. DETAIL: Keep to dates and facts. Discipline that follows protected activity can be retaliation, so do not mention or act on: medical conditions; use of earned sick and safe leave (an employer may not count those absences under an attendance policy that can lead to discipline, Labor & Employment §3-1309(c)); an injury report or workers' compensation claim; a pay complaint; asking about or discussing pay (§3-304.1 bars discipline for asking about or disclosing one's own wages, or discussing another employee's wages when those were disclosed voluntarily, or asking the reason for one's pay); talking with coworkers about pay or conditions (for non-supervisory employees, protected by 29 U.S.C. §157, the National Labor Relations Act); reporting to a government agency; religion, nationality or other protected traits. An employer may adopt a written policy that sets reasonable workday limits on the time, place and manner of pay discussions (§3-304.1(b)) — then discipline only for breaking that limit, not for the discussion. If you have a handbook with steps of progressive discipline, follow it, or the employee may argue it is a promise; this letter says the employer may skip steps. Give the employee a copy and keep a copy.",
