    reviewNote: "CHECK THESE FIRST. (1) Keep the letter short and neutral, with no complaints or accusations, and give the notice the job normally expects (often two weeks). (2) If the employee is leaving because of unpaid wages, harassment, discrimination, a safety problem or because they were pushed out, they should talk to an attorney before sending. (3) Keep a signed or emailed copy and a record of when it was delivered. DETAIL FOLLOWS. A resignation does not need the employer's approval, but give the notice the job normally expects (often two weeks). The employer must pay all wages earned through the last day 'on or before the day on which the employee would have been paid the wages if the employment had not been terminated' (Labor & Employment §3-505(a)); accrued vacation need not be paid only if the employer has a written policy limiting it and told you of its leave benefits when you were hired (§3-505(b)). Unused earned sick and safe leave is not paid out (§3-1304(j)) but is restored if you are rehired within 37 weeks (§3-1304(h)). If you are leaving because of unpaid wages, harassment, discrimination or a safety problem, speak to an attorney before sending — do not put accusations in this letter. Keep a signed or emailed copy.",
