    reviewNote: "CHECK FIRST: (1) Decide which side your client is. The liability-cap box favors the provider, so leave it OFF if your client is the customer. (2) The Services box says exactly what will be done, by when, and what the price covers. (3) Ask for the provider's license and insurance if the work needs them, and get a Form W-9 before paying. (4) Is the provider really independent? Someone who works your client's hours, in the store, under supervision is an employee — use the Employment Agreement instead. (5) Construction or landscaping: tick that box and give the State's classification notice. GET A LAWYER IF: the work is home improvement for a homeowner (the Maryland Home Improvement Law, Business Regulation §8-501, requires contract terms this form does not have, such as the contractor's license number and a Commission notice); the contract is large for your client (for example over $25,000); the provider will handle your client's tax or customer data; or the work could injure someone (food, security, equipment, vehicles). DETAIL: Late interest defaults to 0.5% a month (6% a year, the general Maryland interest cap on loans, Commercial Law §12-102). Title 12's interest limits are written for loans and advances of credit (§12-101); the text does not settle whether they reach an overdue service invoice, so the form stays inside them either way: simple interest, not compounded, on the unpaid invoiced amount only, and no more than 8% a year (0.66% a month) on a written agreement the customer signs (§12-103(a)). Going higher risks forfeiting the greater of three times the excess or $500 (§12-114(b)). There is no separate late fee. If the provider will handle the customer's tax or personal information, add a data-security term and get any separate consent that tax-return rules require (IRC §7216). Contractor status depends on the facts, not the wording. For unemployment insurance the worker must be free of control, customarily in an independent business of the same kind, and the work must be outside the customer's usual course of business or place of business (Labor & Employment §8-205). For workers' compensation a worker is presumed a covered employee unless the employer shows he or she is an independent contractor under the common law (§9-202); paid corporate officers and LLC members can also be covered employees (§9-206). In construction and landscaping, the Workplace Fraud Act (Title 3, Subtitle 9; §3-902) presumes employment unless the provider is an \"exempt person\" or passes a three-part test (§3-903), the customer must give each contractor a written classification notice in English and Spanish when hired (§3-914), and a business-entity provider needs a signed contract with tax and insurance acknowledgments, an affidavit, an SDAT good-standing certificate and proof of licenses (§3-903.1) — switch on the construction/landscaping box to add the acknowledgments. The IRS requires Form 1099-NEC when a business pays a person other than a corporation $2,000 or more in a year for services paid after 2025 ($600 before — Instructions for Forms 1099-MISC and 1099-NEC); get a Form W-9 first. Some trades (for example home improvement, electrical, plumbing) need a Maryland license. The ownership clause assigns the finished work to the customer once paid; it does not make the work a \"work made for hire\". The confidentiality clause carries the full federal whistleblower notice (18 U.S.C. §1833(b)), which the law requires in an agreement with an individual contractor that covers confidential information.",
