    reviewNote: "BEFORE THE CLIENT SIGNS: (1) This sells the real estate only. It does not sell the business, licences or inventory: a store sale also needs the Sale of a Business Agreement (with the bulk-transfer steps for inventory). (2) Fill in the tenant list, zoning and intended use: the buyer's due-diligence cancellation right depends on them, and a lease or tenant surprise is the usual dispute. (3) Every owner must sign, companies through an officer with a title and proof of authority, and a title company or real-estate lawyer must run the title search, deed and closing. DETAIL: Commercial sales carry large tax, zoning, environmental and lease consequences. This agreement sells real property and the personal property you list; it does not sell a business or transfer licenses (tobacco, alcohol, lottery, sales-tax). A store sale usually needs a separate Sale of a Business Agreement and the buyer's own licenses. If the sale includes a business's inventory, Maryland's bulk-transfer rules (Commercial Law Title 6, Subtitle 1; Tax-General §§11-505 and 13-802) may apply to that part — use the Sale of a Business Agreement for it. Notices checked against the statute text (mgaleg.maryland.gov): the Critical Area notice (Real Property §14-117(d)) is in the contract for every sale, word for word; the dewatering notice for Baltimore, Carroll, Frederick and Washington counties (§10-711) applies to any real property and is included (the statute asks for bold and underscored type, which this builder cannot print: bold and underline that block in the Word file); by switches, the private-well testing condition (§10-713), the conservation-easement notice (§10-705) and the agricultural land transfer tax notice (§14-117(e)(20), Tax-Property §13-308; Washington County has its own, §13-504) are added. If part of the property is a home or apartment, the residential notices are added with their exact wording: transfer and recordation tax (§14-117(c)), military noise (§14-117(k)), homestead credit with the State web address https://dat.maryland.gov/homestead (§14-117(n)), assessment appeal (§10-709), the buyer's rights under the disclosure law (§10-702(l)), Anne Arundel land-use (§10-703), Montgomery school boundaries (§10-712) and the flood-risk disclosure that applies from July 1, 2027 (§10-716, Chapter 776 of 2026). The Maryland condominium resale law (§11-135) does not apply to a unit used only for non-residential purposes (§11-135(i)); the association clause says so. Not built in because they depend on facts the form cannot know: special taxing districts (§§10-704, 10-706, 10-707), Prince George's County notices, the broker notices of Business Occupations and Professions Article §§17-405, 17-523 and 17-524 (the broker supplies them) — ask the title company and attach any that applies. Deposit: the kept-deposit clause uses reasonable-estimate wording (Maryland has no statute capping a forfeited deposit on a resale; keep the deposit modest), and if a broker holds the deposit the contract carries the statement Business Occupations and Professions Article §17-505(g) requires. Tax: Tax-General §10-912 non-resident withholding applies to the sale of real property and its associated tangible personal property — the deed cannot be recorded unless the withholding is paid, or each seller certifies Maryland residence (for a company: formed in Maryland, or registered with SDAT) under penalties of perjury, or holds a Comptroller certificate (Form MW506AE, applied for at least 21 days before closing); the settlement agent reports the payment on Form MW506NRS; the Comptroller's rates from July 1, 2025 are 8.75% for non-resident individuals and 8.25% for non-resident entities of the net proceeds, so check the current rate. Federal FIRPTA (Internal Revenue Code §1445): 15% of the price from a foreign seller unless a signed certification of non-foreign status (Treasury Regulation §1.1445-2(b)(2)) is given — it is attached as the last page; a single-member LLC ignored for tax purposes cannot sign it, its owner does. An environmental assessment protects the buyer from federal liability only if it meets the federal standard (a lawyer should confirm this for an industrial or high-value site); the seller-statement survival period is a business choice. The buyer's and seller's company officers must sign with their titles; collect authority documents for the title company.",
