    reviewNote: "CHECK FIRST (3 things): (1) The current Maryland Tenants' Bill of Rights must be uploaded in the document settings (the State issues a new edition every October 1) so that it prints with the lease, and the tenant must be handed the State mold pamphlet at signing. (2) If the home was built before 1978, answer that question honestly: Maryland lead registration, an inspection certificate and MDE's lead papers must be in place before move-in, and the papers must be sent again every two years (put a reminder on the calendar). (3) Deposit (if any) no more than one month's rent (leave the deposit empty for none: the deposit clause and receipt page then drop out), late fee no more than 5% of the rent, returned-payment fee no more than $35 (the app blocks higher amounts); make sure the landlord holds any rental license or registration the city or county requires (the number does not have to be written in the lease) and give the tenant the signed deposit receipt. (4) A SUBLEASE (the landlord is itself a tenant, for example a store tenant renting out the floors above): switch on the sublease question, read the master lease first, and get the owner to sign the consent page BEFORE the new tenant moves in. In Maryland the lead law counts anyone who holds even a leasehold interest as an owner (Environment Article 6-801(o)), so the subletting tenant must register the home, pass the inspection and give the lead papers just like an owner when the home was built before 1950 or before 1978 (6-801(b)); Baltimore City also requires the home to be registered and licensed as a rental (license number is required here). DETAIL FOLLOWS. Drafted from the statute text, not legal advice. Built in (Real Property Article unless noted): a security deposit of no more than one month's rent with a receipt that lists the tenant's rights (8-203, 8-203.1); a late fee of no more than 5% of the unpaid rent (8-208(d)(3)); nothing required to start the lease except the deposit and the first month's rent, so no up-front or non-refundable pet fee and no extra move-in fee (8-208(d)(11)); the current Maryland Tenants' Bill of Rights attached (8-208(c)(4)) and the State mold pamphlet given at signing (8-220(b)); written notice of at least 24 hours before entry, Monday to Saturday 7 a.m. to 7 p.m., delivered by first-class mail with a certificate of mailing, a paper notice on the door, or (only if the tenant elects) email, text or portal, with the emergency exception (8-221, in force since October 1, 2025; Subtitle 2 of Title 8 applies only to residential leases, 8-201(a)); 60 days' landlord notice and 30 days' tenant notice to end a tenancy (8-402(c)); a rent-increase notice at least 90 days ahead for a lease term longer than one month and 60 days ahead for a month-to-month tenancy, by first-class mail with a certificate of mailing or electronically only if the tenant elects (8-209); no lock-outs or utility cut-offs (8-216); no retaliation (8-208.1); no penalty for calling police or emergency services (8-208(d)(10)). Smoke and carbon monoxide alarms (Public Safety Article): the landlord installs, repairs, maintains and replaces required alarms at its own cost, the occupant tests smoke alarms and must not tamper with any alarm, and the tenant reports a failed smoke alarm in writing (certified mail, or hand delivery with a receipt) so the landlord acknowledges and fixes it within 5 calendar days (9-106(c)-(e), 9-105(f)); rental homes need carbon monoxide alarms outside each separate sleeping area and on every level (12-1104(c)); counties may be stricter (9-102(e), 12-1106). Returned-payment fee: kept at or below $35 (Commercial Law 15-802 sets a collection fee of up to $35 on a dishonored check) and counted with any late fee toward the 5% limit, because the statute does not say whether a bank-return fee is a 'late penalty' - this is the safe reading; the app blocks an amount above $35. Monthly pet fee: allowed as a separate monthly charge starting with the second monthly payment (the pet-policy section, 8-210(c), lists 'monthly pet fees'), never a deposit or one-time fee and never for an assistance animal; the landlord must also give a written copy or summary of the pet policy (breed and weight limits, number of pets, every fee, vaccination and insurance rules) with the rental application (8-210(c)). Air conditioning: the Tenants' Bill of Rights says that in a building with 10 or more rental units, air conditioning the landlord has provided (or the lease requires) must cool habitable rooms to 80 degrees from June 1 to September 30; the optional switch adds that promise - this rule was read in the Bill of Rights, not in section text. Lead paint (Environment Article Title 6, Subtitle 8): a rental home built before 1978 is an 'affected property' (6-801) unless it is certified lead-free (6-803(b), 6-804). The landlord must register it with MDE and renew the registration (6-811, 6-812: by December 31 each year unless MDE's regulation sets another schedule; check the MDE registry for the current cycle and fee); at each change of occupancy and before the next tenant moves in, pass the lead-dust test and have an accredited inspector who is not related to the owner inspect and certify it (6-815, 6-818); give the tenant at the start of the tenancy MDE's Notice of Tenant's Rights, MDE's lead poisoning information packet and a copy of the current inspection certificate (6-820(b),(d), 6-823(b)); fix a reported paint defect within 30 days after written notice (6-819(d)); and RE-DELIVER the Notice of Tenant's Rights and the information packet at least every 2 years after last giving them, by certified mail, return receipt requested, or another method MDE approves, with documentation to MDE (6-820(c),(e), 6-823(c),(d)) - so yes, they must be redistributed every two years; put a two-year reminder on the calendar (the app does not send them). Federal rules (40 CFR 745.107, 745.113): the EPA pamphlet, the Lead Warning Statement, disclosure of known lead paint and of available records, and signatures before the tenant is bound, with the signed form kept at least 3 years; exempt are 0-bedroom homes (studios, single rooms) and housing for the elderly or disabled unless a child under 6 lives there, leases of 100 days or less, housing found lead-based-paint free by a certified inspector, and renewals when everything was disclosed before and nothing new is known (745.101, 745.103). Use the 'certified lead-free' answer only with a full lead-free certificate; a partial exemption (interior lead-free, exterior restored) is not enough, so choose 'built before 1978'. MDE's own forms (Notice of Tenant's Rights, packet, inspection certificate) are not reproduced here; download the current ones from MDE. Maryland's lead regulations (COMAR 26.16) were not separately read. Local checklist (not statute-level; check each before signing): the city or county rental license number, any local lead or registration rules (for example Baltimore City or Montgomery County), local rent-increase caps and eviction rules, and local smoke-alarm rules. Binding arbitration, confession of judgment and a jury-trial waiver are deliberately not included (8-208(d)(1),(4)). The app checks that the deposit is no more than one month's rent and the late fee no more than 5%; it does not check the entry notice or the lead certificate number.",
