    description: "A full commercial lease for a store, office or warehouse: definitions, rent and yearly increases, who pays taxes, insurance and common-area costs, operating rules, improvements, insurance, transfer or sale of the business, default, guarantee and renewal.",
    reviewNote: "CHECK FIRST (3 things): (1) Go through the money choices with the client: rent type (who pays taxes, insurance and common-area costs), the yearly increase and the renewal terms; a renewal at 'market rent' needs a way to set it. (2) If the term with renewals can pass seven years, a memorandum of lease must be signed and recorded (Real Property 3-101). (3) A personal guarantee makes the guarantor owe all the rent for the whole term and renewals: the guarantor must understand that before signing. Also confirm the tenant's tobacco, alcohol, lottery and local licenses are in the tenant's own name (they do not pass with a lease). DETAIL FOLLOWS. A commercial lease can bind the parties for years and for large sums; the legal points below were read from the statute text, but the business choices (rent type, exclusive use, personal guarantee, renewal, default periods) are yours to make deliberately, and an attorney is worth paying when the term or the money is large. A renewal written as 'rent at market' needs a way to set that rent. Ending the lease: Real Property Article 8-402(c) covers every tenancy at the end of a stated term and every month-to-month tenancy, not only homes. A landlord must give written notice of intent to terminate 60 days before a written lease for a stated term expires or before a month-to-month tenancy ends, and a tenant gives 30 days. The business-lease reading is not tested in court, so the lease follows it: the landlord promises 60 days' written notice if it wants possession on the end date (put that date on the calendar), the tenant promises 30, and a holdover with the landlord's written consent becomes month to month (8-402(d)). Entry, deposit and late fee: Subtitle 2 of Title 8 applies only to residential leases (8-201(a)), so the 24-hour entry rule (8-221) and the deposit and late-fee caps do not apply to a business lease; 24 hours is kept only as a default. Repossession for a breach other than rent: the landlord must give 30 days' written notice that the tenant is in violation and that the landlord wants to repossess (14 days where there is a clear and imminent danger of serious harm) before filing in District Court (8-402.1(a)); the lease gives the longer of the cure period you choose and 30 days. Unpaid rent can be sued on without that notice (8-401). Distress and self-help: 'distress for rent' (8-301 to 8-306) still exists but is a court action - the landlord files a sworn petition in the District Court, there is a hearing at least 7 days after service, and only then does the sheriff levy on goods (8-302 to 8-305), and only for rent under a written lease for more than 3 months or a tenancy at will or periodic tenancy that has run more than 3 months. It is not seizure by the landlord's own hands, and the statutes read give no right to lock out a business tenant. The lease therefore says possession and goods are taken only through the courts and gives the landlord no right to lock out, cut off utilities or seize or sell the tenant's property; a landlord who wants a security interest in the tenant's inventory or equipment needs a separate security agreement and a UCC filing. Left out on purpose, as business choices rather than gaps: confession of judgment, a fixed liquidated-damages formula for the rest of the term (a penalty if too high), a receiver to run the tenant's business, and making the tenant insure the building's structure - add one only if the landlord insists and you accept the risk. Recording (3-101): a lease, or a memorandum of lease, must be recorded if the first term or any renewal term is longer than seven years; a lease with a first term of seven years or less is exempt only if every renewal term is also seven years or less and a party to the lease can bring it about or prevent it. A memorandum of lease must be signed by every party and state the parties and their addresses, a reference to the lease and its date, the premises 'in the form contained in the lease' (so add the legal description from the deed in the optional box), the start and end dates, and any renewal right with its longest period and exercise dates; the lease itself must be shown to the recording office, which collects any transfer or recording tax. An unrecorded lease is still valid between the parties, against their creditors and against anyone who knows of it or sees the tenant in occupancy. A returned-payment fee is optional and kept at $35 or less (Commercial Law 15-802). The late fee is described as a one-time handling charge, separate from interest; there is no statutory cap for a business lease. Check each local item before signing: the city or county business or rental license, zoning for the use, and any tobacco, alcohol or lottery license (these do not pass with the lease). Written with Maryland in mind and compared with a standard commercial lease form. Companies must sign through an officer with a title; collect authority documents for both sides.",
