    key: "letter_of_intent", title: "Letter of Intent to Buy a Business", category: BUSINESS,
    description: "A short, non-binding letter that records the basic terms before a purchase agreement is written — the business, the assets, the price, the target closing date and what each side expects.",
    reviewNote: "BEFORE THE CLIENT SIGNS: (1) Tell both sides that the letter does NOT bind them to the sale: only a signed purchase agreement does. (2) Confidentiality and the exclusive negotiating period DO bind them if switched on, and so do the general terms (each side pays its own costs); keep the exclusive period short (30 days is the default). (3) Plan the licences, the landlord's consent and the bulk-transfer notice now, because they take the longest. DETAIL: A letter of intent is meant to be non-binding on the sale itself: only the purchase agreement, once signed, binds the parties. The price and terms here are expectations; the purchase agreement can change them. Licenses (tobacco, alcohol, lottery, SNAP/EBT, health) do not transfer to a buyer and the buyer applies in its own name — for example a Maryland lottery license ends when ownership changes (COMAR 36.02.03.15) — so plan the licensing steps early. If the business sells goods from stock (a store, deli, restaurant, alcohol seller), Maryland's bulk-transfer rules (Commercial Law §§6-102, 6-104, 6-105, 6-107, 6-106; Tax-General §§11-505, 13-802, read in full) require the seller's sworn creditor list, a notice to the Comptroller and the creditors at least 10 days before the buyer takes possession or pays, and a holdback of whatever the Comptroller claims for unpaid sales and use tax — a buyer who skips them can be personally liable for that tax — and the landlord's consent to a lease assignment should be planned early too. Equipment and furniture sold with a business are taxable retail sales of tangible personal property; goodwill is not (Tax-General §11-101). Labor & Employment §3-716, which voids some non-competes, covers employment contracts with low-wage workers, certain health-care workers and veterinary staff; it says nothing about the seller of a business, and the letter states that the Seller would agree to the non-compete as seller of the business, not as an employee. The letter also states that neither side must continue the talks, that the buyer's duty to buy would depend on a satisfactory review of the business, and that the confidentiality promise survives the letter.",
