    key: "sales_agreement", title: "Sales Agreement (Goods)", category: BUSINESS,
    description: "A seller sells goods — equipment, stock, vehicles or other items — to a buyer: price and tax, payment, delivery, risk of loss, warranties, title, inspection, claims and remedies.",
    reviewNote: "BEFORE THE CLIENT SIGNS: (1) Decide first whether the Buyer is a consumer (buying for personal, family or household use). If so, switch the consumer question on: the 'as is' choice and the refund-only limit are then not used and the Buyer keeps all legal warranties. (2) For a car, truck, boat or trailer the Seller must also sign over the title (with the odometer statement for a vehicle) and the Buyer pays the titling tax and fees. A consumer sale of a vehicle over 6 model years old with over 60,000 miles needs the Motor Vehicle Administration's form attached. (3) A deposit the Seller keeps is limited to the Seller's real loss. This form is for goods only: for a whole business use the Sale of a Business Agreement. DETAIL: This is for selling goods between businesses or private parties. Checked against the statute text (mgaleg.maryland.gov). Commercial Law §2-316: to exclude the implied warranty of merchantability the writing must mention merchantability and be conspicuous (capital letters here); an exclusion of fitness for a particular purpose must be in a conspicuous writing; 'as is' language excludes all implied warranties. Commercial Law §2-316.1: §2-316 does NOT apply to consumer goods (goods bought mainly for personal, family or household use), and for them any language excluding or modifying the implied warranties or the consumer's remedies is unenforceable — so switch on the consumer question for such a buyer and the 'as is' option and the refund-only limit are not used. The only exception (§2-316.1(4)) is a motor vehicle that must be titled in Maryland, is more than 6 model years old and has been driven more than 60,000 miles, when at the time of sale the seller gives the buyer notice on the form the Motor Vehicle Administration provides (Transportation Article §13-119); the exclusion must then be in writing, mention merchantability and be conspicuous, and EACH exclusion must be separately acknowledged by the buyer's signature. The form handles that case: switch on the consumer question, then the old-vehicle question; the document prints the capital-letter exclusion and two separate buyer signature lines, and you must attach the MVA's form and give it to the buyer at the sale. Commercial Law §2-719: a limit on remedies (refund of the price only) is allowed but, if it fails of its essential purpose, the buyer has the other remedies of the Maryland Uniform Commercial Code; consequential damages may be limited or excluded unless that is unconscionable, but limiting them for personal injury from consumer goods is prima facie unconscionable — the clause therefore keeps personal injury out of the exclusion and is not used in a consumer sale. A kept deposit is limited by §2-718: damages may be fixed in the agreement only at a reasonable amount, and a buyer who defaults is generally entitled to restitution of the excess over the seller's damages (20% of the value or $500, whichever is smaller, if there is no valid fixed amount); the form lets the seller keep a deposit only to the extent of its actual loss. A vehicle, boat or other titled item needs its own signed title transfer, and the buyer normally pays the titling tax and fees to the Motor Vehicle Administration. For selling a whole business use the Sale of a Business Agreement. Sales tax: Maryland generally taxes sales of goods; the buyer pays it unless it has a valid exemption certificate. Written under the Maryland Uniform Commercial Code (Commercial Law Title 2) and",
