    reviewNote: "BEFORE THE CLIENT SIGNS: (1) Fill in exactly what is refunded or kept, by when, and what is handed back (keys, goods, papers). (2) If the Buyer already took over the business, paid a landlord or filed licence or bulk-transfer papers, list each step to undo in the optional box; a bulk-transfer notice that already went to the Comptroller and creditors must be withdrawn in writing. (3) If an escrow agent, broker or attorney holds the deposit, name them and have them confirm the release. DETAIL: A cancellation should say what happens to the money and the property. The document always includes a section that undoes what was done for the sale (license applications, lease assignment, bulk-transfer notices to the Comptroller and creditors, financing statements and liens, vehicle titles, any sales tax paid); use the optional box for anything specific to this sale. If any money is kept, state why — a deposit the seller keeps is enforced only if it is a reasonable estimate of the seller's loss, not a penalty (Maryland has no statute capping it for a business sale; for a sale of goods Commercial Law §2-718 limits it to a reasonable amount). The bulk-transfer notice that may already have gone to the Comptroller and creditors under Commercial Law §§6-105 and 6-107 and Tax-General §11-505 has no statutory withdrawal form: the document has both sides tell each recipient in writing, and return any money held back for them. If a broker, attorney or escrow agent holds the deposit, name them: a broker usually cannot release a deposit without both parties' written agreement, which this document provides. The release does not cover fraud. For ending other contracts use the Contract Termination Agreement.",
