    description: "The letter that asks the IRS to close a business's account and cancel its EIN after the business closes, is sold or is dissolved, with the details the IRS asks for.",
    reviewNote: "BEFORE SENDING: (1) Answer 'all returns filed and taxes paid' Yes only if it is true; the IRS will not close the account until every final return is filed and every tax is paid, and if you answer No the letter prints a DO NOT SEND warning. (2) Send it only if the business itself is ending. If a company only sold its assets and still exists, do not close its account. (3) Enclose a copy of the CP 575 or 147C notice if you have it, have an owner or officer sign, mail it to Cincinnati, and keep a copy, the mailing receipt and the IRS reply. DETAIL: Checked against the IRS page 'Closing a business' (irs.gov): the letter must give the complete legal name, the EIN, the business address and the reason, and you should enclose a copy of the notice the IRS sent when it assigned the EIN (CP 575 or 147C); mail both to Internal Revenue Service, Cincinnati, OH 45999. The IRS says it cannot close the account until every required return has been filed and every tax owed has been paid — so file the final returns first: the final income-tax return with the 'final return' box checked (Schedule C for a sole proprietor; Form 1065 and final K-1s; Form 1120 or 1120-S and final K-1s; Form 966 when a corporation adopts a plan to dissolve), final Form 941 or 944 with the closed-business box and the date of the last wages and a statement naming who keeps the payroll records, final Form 940 with the final-return box, W-2s and W-3, Form 8027 if there are tips, and Forms 1099-NEC (and 1096) for contractor payments above the reporting threshold ($600 before 2026, $2,000 for payments made after 2025). Also close the state side: Maryland Form MW506FR (withholding) and Form 202FR (sales and use tax) are in Government Forms, plus the Maryland unemployment-insurance final report, and the SDAT dissolution if the entity is ending. An older version of this letter used the 'MS 6055, Kansas City, MO 64108' address; the IRS page now gives Cincinnati, OH 45999. The letter must be signed by an owner or officer. Keep a copy and the IRS's reply, which confirms the account is closed. The IRS keeps an EIN as the business's permanent number and does not reuse it; its help pages also say it deactivates rather than deletes a number, so the letter asks to close the account and cancel or deactivate the EIN.",
