    key: "business_sale_agreement", title: "Sale of a Business Agreement", category: BUSINESS,
    description: "Selling or buying a whole store or business — price, what is included, inventory, lease, licenses, closing conditions and what the seller must not do after the sale.",
    reviewNote: "BEFORE THE CLIENT SIGNS: (1) The 10-day bulk-transfer notice to the Comptroller and the creditors must go out before any money reaches the Seller or the Buyer takes possession (the agreement moves the closing date if the 10 days have not run). Name a lawyer or escrow agent to hold the deposit. (2) The Buyer needs its own licences (tobacco, lottery, SNAP, health, sales tax, alcohol): none transfers, and no restricted stock changes hands before the Buyer is licensed. (3) Get the landlord's written consent to the lease assignment and agree the price split (equipment, inventory, goodwill) with the tax preparer. A lawyer should review a high-value sale. DETAIL: Checked against the statute text (mgaleg.maryland.gov): (1) Bulk transfer (Commercial Law §§6-101 to 6-111; Tax-General §§11-505, 13-802). It applies to a business whose principal business is selling merchandise from stock — a store, deli, restaurant, alcohol seller — when a major part of its inventory is sold other than in the ordinary course; equipment counts only when sold together with inventory (§6-102). The buyer must (a) require the seller's signed and sworn list of creditors and a schedule of the property and keep both six months after the transfer, or file them with the circuit court clerk (§6-104); (b) send a notice at least 10 days before taking possession or paying, whichever is first, by personal delivery or registered or certified mail, to the Comptroller (always, even if no tax is known to be owed), the listed creditors and anyone else known to claim against the seller (§§6-105, 6-107; Tax-General §11-505) — so name an attorney or escrow agent to hold the deposit; (c) apply the price to the debts on the list or filed in writing within 30 days after the notice was mailed, holding back disputed sums and paying pro rata if the price is short (§6-106); and (d) withhold the amount of any claim the Comptroller files for unpaid sales and use tax (§11-505(c)). A buyer who skips the notice or the holdback is personally liable to the State for the seller's sales and use tax, interest and penalties (§13-802), and the transfer is ineffective against the seller's creditors. The statute has no Comptroller clearance certificate for a bulk sale: the agreement therefore asks the seller for a written account-status statement from the Comptroller and lets you set a tax holdback amount (optional question). The form notice and creditor list are attached as the last pages. The Comptroller's own bulk-sales guidance page could not be read (it needs a browser) — call the Comptroller's bulk-sales line for the mailing address before sending. Commercial Law §6-111 says no action or levy under the bulk-transfer title may start more than six months after the buyer takes possession, but the Comptroller has argued that this does not limit its sales-tax claim, so do not rely on it. (2) Sales tax (Tax-General §§11-101(h), (k), 11-102, 11-209, 11-104): the equipment, furniture and other movable items are tangible personal property and their sale is a taxable retail sale, generally at 6% of the taxable price; the only casual-and-isolated-sale exemption covers a seller that regularly does NOT sell such property and a price under $1,000, so it does not help a store; inventory the buyer will resell in the form it receives is not a retail sale (the Comptroller handles this with a resale certificate); goodwill and other intangibles are not tangible personal property. (3) IRS Form 8594 and how the price is split, including any amount paid for the non-compete. (4) Licenses — tobacco, alcohol, lottery, SNAP/EBT, health and sales-tax registrations do not transfer and the buyer must apply in the buyer's own name, sometimes well before closing (a lottery license ends when ownership changes — COMAR 36.02.03.15; the local liquor board must approve a new alcohol licensee); no tobacco, alcohol or lottery stock should change hands before the buyer is licensed. (5) Non-compete: Labor & Employment §3-716 makes a non-compete void only in an employment contract or similar agreement with a worker earning up to 150% of the State minimum wage, a licensed direct-patient-care health worker earning up to $350,000, or a licensed veterinary practitioner or technician; it says nothing about the seller of a business, so a sale-of-business promise is judged by the usual reasonableness test. To be safe under either reading, the clause is given by the seller as seller of the business and its goodwill (not as an employee and not as a condition of a job), each limit is a separate promise a court may narrow, and the optional owner line binds the owner personally. If the seller will also work for the buyer, keep the non-compete out of any employment paper for a worker at or below 150% of the minimum wage. The years and miles are a business choice. (6) Employee, payroll and unemployment-insurance accounts. (7) Vehicles need their own title transfer. (8) Deposit: Maryland has no statute capping a deposit the seller keeps in a business sale; it is enforced only if it is a reasonable estimate of the seller's loss, so keep it modest — the clause says so in those words. Not copied from it: letting the buyer operate for a while under the seller's own licenses, permits or tax registrations (a license can be used only by the person it was issued to — the optional transition clause covers ordinary service accounts only), its fixed one-mile / 3-year non-compete, and its waiver of every defense to the non-compete. The statements, conditions, as-is clause and three-year claim period are business choices — have an attorney confirm them for this deal if the price is large.",
