    body: `PERSONAL GUARANTY

Date: {{effectiveDate}}

1. THE GUARANTY
In consideration of {{creditor}} ("Creditor") extending or continuing credit to {{borrower}} ("Borrower"), and for other value received, {{guarantor}}{{#if guarantorAddress}} of {{guarantorAddress}}{{/if}} ("Guarantor") personally and unconditionally guarantees to the Creditor the full and on-time payment of the following obligation of the Borrower (the "Obligation"): {{obligation}}

2. HOW MUCH
{{#if limit=all}}The Guarantor is responsible for everything the Borrower owes under the Obligation, including interest, fees and the Creditor's costs of collecting from the Guarantor: court costs and reasonable attorney's fees actually incurred (not a percentage of the debt), to the extent the law allows.{{else}}The Guarantor is responsible for up to {{cap}} of principal and interest owed under the Obligation, plus the Creditor's court costs and reasonable attorney's fees actually incurred (not a percentage of the debt) in collecting from the Guarantor, to the extent the law allows.{{/if}}

3. NATURE OF THE PROMISE
This is a guaranty of payment, not only of collection: if the Borrower does not pay when due, the Creditor may demand payment from the Guarantor without first suing the Borrower or using any security, and the Guarantor will pay within 10 days after the Creditor's written demand. The guaranty continues until the Obligation is paid in full, even if the Creditor extends time, renews the Obligation, changes its terms with the Borrower, or accepts partial payments{{#if noIncrease}}, but the Guarantor is not responsible for any increase in the amount owed, the rent or the interest rate that the Guarantor has not agreed to in writing{{/if}}. It also continues if the Borrower's business changes owner, name or form. This guaranty covers only the Obligation described above and not any other debt of the Borrower. The Guarantor understands that if the Borrower does not pay, the Creditor can sue the Guarantor and collect from the Guarantor's own money and property.

{{#if revocable}}4. ENDING THE GUARANTY FOR FUTURE CREDIT
The Guarantor may end this guaranty for credit extended, and amounts that become owing, after the Creditor receives the Guarantor's written notice of ending. The Guarantor remains responsible for everything the Borrower owed, or the Creditor was already committed to provide, when the notice was received, together with interest and costs on it and renewals of it. Ending this guaranty does not affect any other guarantor.

{{/if}}5. WHAT THE GUARANTOR GIVES UP
The Guarantor waives notice of default, demand, presentment and protest, and the right to require the Creditor to pursue the Borrower or any collateral first. The Guarantor also waives all defenses based on suretyship or impairment of collateral, including any discharge under Commercial Law §3-605 because the Creditor extends the time for payment, agrees to change the Obligation, or releases, fails to perfect or otherwise impairs collateral or another guarantor's obligation. The Guarantor's obligation is not reduced or ended by the Borrower's bankruptcy or discharge, or by any lack of capacity or authority of the Borrower. The Guarantor keeps the defenses that the Obligation has been paid or performed and that it is void or unlawful. This guaranty does not waive the Creditor's duty to act in good faith and to follow the law on notice, sale and accounting for any repossessed collateral, which the law does not let a guarantor give up in advance. If a payment to the Creditor is later returned or set aside, this guaranty is reinstated. Until the Creditor is paid in full, the Guarantor waives rights of subrogation, reimbursement and contribution against the Borrower. The Guarantor is responsible for keeping informed about the Borrower's finances and the Creditor has no duty to report on them, but on the Guarantor's written request the Creditor will tell the Guarantor in writing the amount then owed under the Obligation and whether the Borrower is in default.

{{#if extraClauses}}6. ADDITIONAL TERMS
{{extraClauses}}

{{/if}}7. GENERAL
The Guarantor confirms that the Guarantor has received and read the documents that create the Obligation and has had the chance to ask a lawyer about this guaranty before signing.{{#if consumerLoan}} The Guarantor also confirms that, before signing, the Guarantor received and read the separate "Notice to Cosigner" page required by federal rule (16 CFR §444.3).{{/if}} This guaranty binds the Guarantor's heirs and estate, benefits the Creditor's successors and assigns, may be changed only in writing signed by the Guarantor and the Creditor, and is governed by the laws of the State of {{state}}, whose courts may settle any dispute under it. Notices must be in writing. Delay in enforcing a right is not a waiver of it. If more than one person signs a guaranty of this Obligation, each is liable for the whole amount. This guaranty may be signed in counterparts, including electronically. If a term cannot be enforced the rest stays in force.{{#if underSeal}} The Guarantor signs this guaranty under seal, intending it to be a sealed instrument, and the mark "(SEAL)" next to the Guarantor's signature is the Guarantor's seal.{{/if}}

GUARANTOR: {{guarantor}}
Signature{{#if underSeal}} (SEAL){{/if}}: ______________________________   Date: ______________

CREDITOR (accepting): {{creditor}}
Signature: ______________________________   Date: ______________
Printed name: ______________________   Title (if a company): ______________{{#if consumerLoan}}

[page break]
Notice to Cosigner

You are being asked to guarantee this debt. Think carefully before you do. If the borrower doesn't pay the debt, you will have to. Be sure you can afford to pay if you have to, and that you want to accept this responsibility.

You may have to pay up to the full amount of the debt if the borrower does not pay. You may also have to pay late fees or collection costs, which increase this amount.

The creditor can collect this debt from you without first trying to collect from the borrower. The creditor can use the same collection methods against you that can be used against the borrower, such as suing you, garnishing your wages, etc. If this debt is ever in default, that fact may become a part of your credit record.

This notice is not the contract that makes you liable for the debt.{{/if}}`,
