    reviewNote: "CHECK THESE FIRST. (1) Offices that have their own form still need it signed: Maryland Form 548 (Comptroller), the unemployment-insurance employer power of attorney, the FNS Letter of Representation (SNAP), IRS Form 2848 / 8821. This document does not replace them. (2) The owner or officer signs anything the form says an owner, officer or principal must sign (for example the SDAT annual report); the firm prepares and submits it. (3) Tick only what this client needs. This power never lets the firm touch the client's bank account or login. DETAIL FOLLOWS. Who may sign what, as read from the primary sources. (1) MARYLAND COMPTROLLER: tax accounts require its own Form 548; the Comptroller's website says it no longer accepts IRS Form 2848 for Maryland taxes, and an officer, partner or other authorized person signs Form 548 for a business while the representative (the firm) also completes and signs the declaration (read from the Comptroller's site and form summary; the PDF itself could not be downloaded here, so check the current form). The Combined Registration Application: the signer rules could not be retrieved; have the owner or officer sign and let the firm prepare and submit it. The IRS uses Form 2848 / 8821. (2) STATE DEPARTMENT OF ASSESSMENTS AND TAXATION: the annual report / personal property return (Form 1) has a signature block for a 'Corporate Officer or Principal of Entity', who declares under penalty of perjury, and a separate block for the 'Firm or Individual, other than taxpayer, preparing' it, so the firm prepares and submits it and an officer or principal signs it (read on the form). For an LLC, Corporations and Associations §4A-206(b) lets an authorized person sign articles of amendment, merger, dissolution and similar certificates through an attorney-in-fact and says the power need not be notarized or filed (articles of organization are signed by an individual the founders authorize, §4A-206(a)(1)); for a corporation, articles of amendment and similar filings are signed by named officers or, if the bylaws or a board resolution allow it, another officer or agent (§1-301(a)). (3) USDA FOOD AND NUTRITION SERVICE (SNAP): the federal retailer rule (7 CFR 278.1) says only that the application is filed 'as prescribed by FNS' and does not name the signer; FNS's own Letter of Representation is signed by the store owner (named as in FNS ownership records) and by each designated representative (read from a published copy of the form, not the FNS page). (4) The Maryland unemployment-insurance office uses its own employer power-of-attorney form through its portal. (5) County and city offices, health departments and the tobacco, lottery and alcohol licensing offices: no general rule was found. The safe course built into this document: the firm prepares and submits, and the owner or officer signs anything the form says must be signed by an owner, officer, principal or partner (section on signing); call the office first and bring the original of this power. The 'firm may sign' switch is for routine forms and letters. MARYLAND POWER-OF-ATTORNEY STATUTE (Estates and Trusts Title 17, read verbatim): a 'principal' is 'an individual' (§17-101(j)), and §17-109(b) excludes a power to prepare, sign or file documents for an entity with a government body or third party (item 7) and a power on a government-prescribed form (item 4). So for a company or other entity, which is the normal case, Title 17 does not apply: no witnesses are required, the copy and good-faith sentences are a request and an agreement by the business (an office may still refuse), and the notary block is there because some offices ask for an acknowledged power. If the 'business' is a sole proprietor (one individual, no company), Title 17 does apply to the part of the power that is not on a government form, so tick the sole-proprietor switch to add the two adult-witness lines (§17-110(a)). The power ends one year after signing unless an end date is set; some offices want a recent date. The firm's own telephone numbers and e-mail address pre-filled below should be checked for currency; whether to keep the named representatives pre-filled is a decision for the firm. This is drafting help, not legal advice.",
